A||||7||OilStumped||Tires
Z||000000||OilStumped||05-01-99||09:57 PM||||OK...got another prob for ya.  My 85 2m6 had custom rims on it, wir 195/70 14 tires on it.  I swapped them tires and all onto my 85 GT, which is supposed to take 215/60 14's.  When it rains outside, and I drive thru a puddle, the car fishtails.  Is this because of the narrower tires or do I have a bigger problem on my hands here?
Z||000001||theogre||05-01-99||10:01 PM||||there are a number of reasons the car could hydro plane.... narrower tire, shallower tread, harder tread compound, poor tread water displacement,,,,,,,,,,,,<p>you could check the spec,,, usually 1 215 isn't wider than 1 195 at the tread by all that much.... remember,,, the number isn't the tread width... it's the width of the cross section of the tire. tread&lt;cross section in most cases.
Z||000002||OilStumped||05-01-99||10:06 PM||||Yeah...the tires are pretty worn out, cause I had to change my tranny, and I drove it for a while without getting the rear end aligned...yeah, stupid I know, but I had no other wheels to get me around.  I'll try new tires the right size and see what happens.  Good thing I work at a tire shop...haha
Z||000003||Pretender||05-03-99||01:04 AM||||Don't all gt's come w/ 15 inch wheels?. it would be better to replace the tires on the 15" rims, they might cost alittle more but it's worth it. You'll get better traction from  wider tires/rims. your speedo gear ratio might be off a little to.
Z||000004||theogre||05-03-99||07:30 AM||||no... allot of GT cars only had the 14" AL wheel..... 15 was an option only on the later years I think.
Z||000005||Eric||05-03-99||01:12 PM||||Narrow tires are better than wide tires in the wet because they displace less water.  Have you ever seen an indy car or f1 race in wet conditions?  The cars are spinning all over the place.  The pace car could easily outcorner the race cars because it has narrower tires.  However, narrow tires comprimise dry weather traction and unless you live under water, I would have to say "wider is better".
Z||000006||Patrick||05-03-99||02:18 PM||||Eric,<p>There's more to it than just the width of the tires. You haven't mentioned anything at all about <b>tread</b> design. Sure, if the Indy cars have their "<b>slicks</b>" on, of course they'll slip all over the place on a wet road. That's why the race teams have a different set of tires to use on wet pavement. These tires aren't narrower, but they do have treads to help <b>displace</b> the water.<p>I say go for wide tires with good tread design. I live on the we(s)t coast of Canada. Driving on "slicks" is <b>not</b> an option here.:)<p>
Z||000007||Eric||05-03-99||05:59 PM||||Given that the tires have the same tread pattern with the same depth of tread, the narrower tire will reach a higher speed before hydroplaning.  It is just like water skis.  If you use wide skis, it will take less speed to stand up than if you ski barefoot.  The example with the Indy cars was correct as long as the pace car and the race cars were both using treaded tires.<p>Tire manufacturers have been working with this issue for years.  Dunlop has introduced the SP 9000 ( a high performance tire with a V shaped tread element ) to eliminate the problem of hydoplaning for wide tires.  The tread sweeps water toward the outer shoulder instead of twisting it though a maze of sectioned rubber.  This sweeping motion allows the tire to cut through the water, allowing more rubber to stay in contact with the road and makes the car behave like it was on much narrower tires.  Unfortunatly for Fiero owners, the Dunlops only come in 16, 17, and 18 inches, but there are still a number of good comprimise tires on the market.<p>I just bought a set of Yokohama A509s.  205 60 15 in front, 225 60 15 in back.  The first time I drove on these was through atleast an inch of standing water left by a brief thunderstorm.  They handled perfectly.  In fact, I misjudged a driveway and drove into a large ditch complete with a storm drain.  It was so deep, I scraped the frame on the way out!
